and capture raw output\n- Design the result structure (per-benchmark scores, run metadata: cost/duration/tokens/GPU time per HLR-8, suite + version identity)\n- Validate the structure against Model Registry custom properties (type/size constraints, queryability)\n- Confirm behavior under benchmark-suite version bumps (versioned suite identity in the schema)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Prototype persists real MMLU results to a Model Registry model version's custom properties and reads them back\n- Schema records suite identity and version so results remain interpretable after a suite bump\n- Recommendation accepted... by Model Registry + TrustyAI teams? Keep: \"Schema recommendation documented and agreed with the Model Registry team\"\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-3, HLR-8 (schema defines what is persisted); enables HLR-5 display and HLR-7 provider results.\n\nE003 body:\n\n# Investigation: benchmark dataset licensing for disconnected redistribution\n\n## Description\nDetermine which standard benchmark suites (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, and other lm-eval tasks under consideration) can be redistributed with the platform in disconnected environments, and which must be fetched by the customer.\n\n## Scope\n- License review of candidate standard suites and their datasets\n- Classification per suite: redistributable vs customer-fetched, with license terms cited\n- Identification of fallback delivery mechanism for customer-fetched suites\n- Legal confirmation of redistribution determinations (Red Hat legal) — as part of deliverable? \"coordinate legal review\" — this makes it partly human. OK.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Each candidate suite classified with cited license terms\n- Redistribution determinations confirmed by legal\n- List of out-of-the-box (bundled) suites vs customer-fetched suites produced — input to the suite availability epic\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (determines which suites ship out of the box)\n\nE004 body:\n\n# Out-of-the-box standard benchmark suites\n\n## Description\nDeliver standard benchmark suites out of the box so users can select them when launching an evaluation, per the redistribution determinations...\n\n## Scope\n- Package the suites determined redistributable (e.g., MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag exposed through lm-eval task names) for disconnected delivery\n- Provide the customer-fetched delivery mechanism for suites that cannot be redistributed\n- Suite catalog surfaced to the launch flow (execution path + Dashboard selection)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A user can launch an evaluation against each bundled suite in a disconnected environment without external network access\n- Customer-fetched suites can be supplied by the user and run through the same flow\n- Suite catalog identifies suite name and version... \n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (primary)\n- Supports HLR-1 (launch against a selected suite)\n\nE005 body:\n\n# Dashboard evaluation experience: launch, run status, scores, and version comparison\n\n## Description\nDashboard integration for the evaluation capability: launch evaluations of served models (KServe endpoints or Llama Stack distributions) against selected suites, view evaluation runs and status, per-benchmark scores, and side-by-side comparison of two model versions.\n\n## Scope\n- Evaluation navigation section: runs list, status, per-benchmark scores\n- Launch flow: select served model, select benchmark suite (standard or custom), launch\n- Side-by-side comparison view of two model versions' results\n- Reads results via Kubernetes CRs / Model Registry properties per the agreed result schema\n- Feature flag/gating via OdhDashboardConfig? That's implementation detail — arch context mentions per-component feature flags. I can mention \"feature-flagged enablement consistent with existing Dashboard component integrations\" — that's pattern-level, fine.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- User can launch an evaluation of a served model against a selected suite from the Dashboard, and see run status\n- Evaluation runs display per-benchmark scores\n- User can select two model versions and compare results side by side\n- Results render correctly per the canonical result schema\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (launch UI), HLR-5\n\nE006 body:\n\n# Evaluation step in Data Science Pipelines\n\n## Description\nEnable evaluations to be embedded as a step in a Data Science Pipeline so promotion flows can gate on scores.\n\n## Scope\n- Pipeline step/component that launches an evaluation through the platform execution path and exposes results/scores as pipeline outputs\n- Works with KFP v2 SDK (pattern per existing components)... arch context: KFP v2 REST API and SDK\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A pipeline run can include an evaluation step that executes against a served model and completes with per-benchmark scores available as outputs\n- A downstream step can gate on score thresholds... \"promotion flows gate on scores\" — AC: pipeline can branch/gate on the evaluation step's scores\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-6\n\nE007 body:\n\n# Llama Stack eval provider backed by the platform evaluation service\n\n## Description\nWire a production eval provider into the platform's Llama Stack distribution so agents/apps built on Llama Stack get evaluation results from the platform evaluation capability.\n\n## Scope\n- Implement the eval provider plugin in the platform Llama Stack distribution, backed by the platform evaluation execution path selected by the execution-engine investigation\n- Return results in the canonical result schema\n- Configuration on LlamaStackDistribution? — mention provider config surface\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A Llama Stack app/agent invoking the eval provider API receives results consistent with running the evaluation directly on the platform\n- Provider is available in the platform distribution (not a manual add-on)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-7\n\nE008 (docs) body:\n\n# Evaluation workflow documentation\n\n## Description\nProduct documentation 