pic Boundaries (2/2)\nComponent/team tuples produce separate epics; the multiple TrustyAI epics are\ncorrectly separated along logical-change axes (E001/E003 are Investigations,\nto which the boundary rule does not apply; E004 suite packaging vs. the branch\nexecution epics are different logical changes; Rule 15 same-repo parallel is\nrespected with no edge between them). No scope duplication: E004's suite\ncatalog and E005's launch UI are complementary (data provider vs. renderer),\nnot two epics building the same surface. No work-product type mixing — E008 is\npure docs-authoring. One minor concern: BRANCH-B-E009 builds a greenfield\nevaluation service carrying the full functional scope (service,\nlaunch/status/results interface, custom tasks, persistence, cost metadata,\nprovider backing) in a single epic, which risks exceeding ~2 weeks; if outcome\nB is selected, the branch should be decomposed further at resolution time.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure (E001 selects\nbetween branch alternatives; E002 defines the data contract; E003 determines\nthe suite catalog scope), and every Implementation produces a concrete\nartifact. Every `gated_by` target is a direct dependency; every gate has both\n`action` and a substantive `fallback_approach`. One minor inconsistency:\nE005, E007, and both branch epics list Investigation E002 in `dependencies`\nand the DAG justification labels the E002 edges \"true gate (rewrite)\", yet\n`gated_by` (single-valued in the schema) records only E001 — the E002 gate\nrelationship survives only in prose (`fallback_approach` text). Since dual\ngating cannot be expressed in frontmatter, the actionable fix is to label the\nE002 edges as Rule 13 data-contract dependencies rather than \"true gates\", so\nthe justification and frontmatter agree.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout: Investigations carry\n`investigation_signals` (with `ai_signals: null`), Implementations carry\n`ai_signals`; all ten ai-signals files exist and their rationales match the\nfrontmatter values. Investigation signal values are well justified (e.g.\nE003's `human_judgment_required: -1` for legal confirmation of licensing;\nE001's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` with only peripheral validation\nneeding a live cluster). Arithmetic and thresholds not checked per\ninstructions. One minor consistency note: `open_questions` is scored -1 for\nE005/E006/E007 (gated unknowns \"change the implementation approach\") but 0\nfor E004 and both branch epics (gated unknowns \"resolved by the gating\ninvestigation\"). The implicit distinction — E001/E002 outcomes change the\nconsumers' implementation approach while E003's outcome changes E004's\ndeliverable content, and branch epics are conditioned on a settled outcome —\nis defensible, but the rationales should state it explicitly so the scoring\nbasis is uniform.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs are\npresent: E008 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review AC (Rule 24);\nBRANCH-B-E009 carries the conditional \"build pipeline green\" AC for the\nnew-repo case (Rule 25 pattern); no replacement epics exist so Rule 23 is\nN/A. No AC misfiles an external schedule constraint as a completion\ncondition, and no epic body references sibling epics by draft ID —\ninvestigations are referenced by name throughout.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is covered: every HLR maps to epic scope, the three open\nquestions each resolve via a dedicated Investigation, out-of-scope items\n(arena-style evaluation, automatic promotion) are correctly excluded (E006\nexplicitly keeps humans in the promotion decision), and the two bounded\noutcomes of open question 1 are both covered by branch files. Strategy-level\ncontext is carried forward (four health warnings capture the missing strategy\nsections, the passive Model Registry assumption, the licensing scope trap,\nand the branch-B repo risk). Cross-epic consistency holds for E002's schema\nfields (scores, suite identity/version, run metadata) across E005, E007, and\nthe branch epics. One minor gap tied to the Criterion 2 defect: E002's AC\n(\"shared with the teams building the execution path, Dashboard views, and\nLlama Stack provider\") and its \"Enables\" traceability (HLR-5, HLR-7) omit the\npipelines consumer (HLR-6/E006) — the same omission as the missing\nE002 → E006 edge.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":62507,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-86c22061e5140989","agent_id":"a48e50b94a679b3d6","session_id":"a8243e44-1386-4db8-ba04-4dee4477cf9e","uuid":"e99619f8-57e9-4560-89c9-276e3aa89a43","timestamp":"2026-10-22T17:53:15.133Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
