 HLR-5 (P1) shares the\n71\tDashboard evaluation surface with HLR-1 (P0) in E005; HLR-4 (P1) and HLR-8 (P2)\n72\tshare the execution/run-recording surface with the P0 work in the branch epics\n73\t— none is an independently shippable surface, so no priority collapse.\n74\t\n75\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n76\tNo circular dependencies; every edge has a rule citation; the Mermaid diagram\n77\t(dep→dependent arrows) and frontmatter `dependencies` match exactly in both\n78\tdirections; branch files are structurally correct; the E004↔execution\n79\tsuites relationship is correctly handled as a Rule 13 configuration reference\n80\t(AC gate, not an edge); konflux-onboarding parallelism rules are not in play.\n81\tHowever, there is one critical missing edge:\n82\t\n83\t**E002 → E006 is missing.** E006's scope is to \"expose per-benchmark scores and\n84\tpass/fail against configurable thresholds as step outputs\" — the pipeline step\n85\tconsumes the canonical result schema that E002 produces, exactly the\n86\tdata-contract dependency for which E002 → E005 and E002 → E007 edges exist\n87\t(the DAG justification itself states \"the result schema is the data contract\n88\tthe Dashboard display/comparison, the Llama Stack provider results, and the\n89\tpersistence code build against\" — the pipeline step's score outputs are\n90\tomitted from that list). A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a\n91\tcritical issue: E006 could be scheduled and built against an unsettled result\n92\tformat while its sibling consumers are correctly gated. Correction: add\n93\t`RHAISTRAT-9002-E002` to E006's `dependencies` and to the summary DAG diagram\n94\tand justification table.\n95\t\n96\tObservation (not scored, inherent to the branch rules): E008's Rule 11\n97\tdependency set cannot include the conditional execution-core branch epics, so\n98\tdocs coverage of the execution workflow rests on E005/E006/E007, which\n99\tthemselves gate only on E001. This is the prescribed shape per the\n100\tconditional-branch rules and requires no change.\n101\t\n102\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n103\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics; the multiple TrustyAI epics are\n104\tcorrectly separated along logical-change axes (E001/E003 are Investigations,\n105\tto which the boundary rule does not apply; E004 suite packaging vs. the branch\n106\texecution epics are different logical changes; Rule 15 same-repo parallel is\n107\trespected with no edge between them). No scope duplication: E004's suite\n108\tcatalog and E005's launch UI are complementary (data provider vs. renderer),\n109\tnot two epics building the same surface. No work-product type mixing — E008 is\n110\tpure docs-authoring. One minor concern: BRANCH-B-E009 builds a greenfield\n111\tevaluation service carrying the full functional scope (service,\n112\tlaunch/status/results interface, custom tasks, persistence, cost metadata,\n113\tprovider backing) in a single epic, which risks exceeding ~2 weeks; if outcome\n114\tB is selected, the branch should be decomposed further at resolution time.\n115\t\n116\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n117\tAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure (E001 selects\n118\tbetween branch alternatives; E002 defines the data contract; E003 determines\n119\tthe suite catalog scope), and every Implementation produces a concrete\n120\tartifact. Every `gated_by` target is a direct dependency; every gate has both\n121\t`action` and a substantive `fallback_approach`. One minor inconsistency:\n122\tE005, E007, and both branch epics list Investigation E002 in `dependencies`\n123\tand the DAG justification labels the E002 edges \"true gate (rewrite)\", yet\n124\t`gated_by` (single-valued in the schema) records only E001 — the E002 gate\n125\trelationship survives only in prose (`fallback_approach` text). Since dual\n126\tgating cannot be expressed in frontmatter, the actionable fix is to label the\n127\tE002 edges as Rule 13 data-contract dependencies rather than \"true gates\", so\n128\tthe justification and frontmatter agree.\n129\t\n130\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n131\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout: Investigations carry\n132\t`investigation_signals` (with `ai_signals: null`), Implementations carry\n133\t`ai_signals`; all ten ai-signals files exist and their rationales match the\n134\tfrontmatter values. Investigation signal values are well justified (e.g.\n135\tE003's `human_judgment_required: -1` for legal confirmation of licensing;\n136\tE001's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` with only peripheral validation\n137\tneeding a live cluster). Arithmetic and thresholds not checked per\n138\tinstructions. One minor consistency note: `open_questions` is scored -1 for\n139\tE005/E006/E007 (gated unknowns \"change the implementation approach\") but 0\n140\tfor E004 and both branch epics (gated unknowns \"resolved by the gating\n141\tinvestigation\"). The implicit distinction — E001/E002 outcomes change the\n142\tconsumers' implementation approach while E003's outcome changes E004's\n143\tdeliverable content, and branch epics are conditioned on a settled outcome —\n144\tis defensible, but the rationales should state it explicitly so the scoring\n145\tbasis is uniform.\n146\t\n147\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n148\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs are\n149\tpresent: E008 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review AC (Rule 24);\n150\tBRANCH-B-E009 carries the conditional \"build pipeline green\" AC for the\n151\tnew-repo case (Rule 25 pattern); no replacement epics exist so Rule 23 is\n152\tN/A. No AC misfiles an external schedule constraint as a completion\n153\tcondition, and no epic body references sibling epics by draft ID —\n154\tinvestigations are referenced by name throughout.\n155\t\n156\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n157\tAll strategy scope is covered: every HLR maps to epic scope, the three open\n158\tquestions each resolve via a dedicated Investigation, out-of-scope items\n159\t(arena-style evaluation, automatic promotion) are correctly excluded (E006\n160\texplicitly keeps humans in the promotion decision), and the two bounded\n161\toutcomes of open question 1 are both covered by branch files. Strategy-level\n162\tcontext is carried forward (four health warnings capture the missing strategy\n163\tsections, the passive Model Registry assumption, the licensing scope trap,\n164\tand the branch-B repo risk). Cross-epic consistency holds for E002's schema\n165\tfields (scores, suite identity/version, run metadata) across E005, E007, and\n166\tthe branch epics. One minor gap tied to the Criterion 2 defect: E002's AC\n167\t(\"shared with the teams building the execution path, Dashboard views, and\n168\tLlama Stack provider\") and its \"Enables\" traceability (HLR-5, HLR-7) omit the\n169\tpipelines consumer (HLR-6/E006) — the same omission as the missing\n170\tE002 → E006 edge.\n171\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83314622072566be","agent_id":"aaf5de986d62553a8","session_id":"a8243e44-1386-4db8-ba04-4dee4477cf9e","uuid":"9c53b93f-3c23-4688-b492-9d6cd6d3470c","timestamp":"2026-10-22T17:54:13.550Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7fa1ba42680e0b0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
