{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9480b7e6d2755242","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-9002 — Integrated GenAI model evaluation for RHOAI\n10\t\n11\tTriage: not below-threshold (5 affected components, 4 distinct teams, P0–P2 scope\n12\tspanning execution, persistence, UI, pipelines, and provider integration) and not\n13\tdocs-only. Full multi-epic decomposition applies.\n14\t\n15\t## Epic List\n16\t\n17\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n18\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E001 | Investigation: evaluation execution engine — extend LMEvalJob or new evaluation service | Investigation | TrustyAI | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E002 | Investigation: canonical evaluation result schema for Model Registry persistence | Investigation | Model Registry | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E003 | Investigation: benchmark dataset licensing for disconnected redistribution | Investigation | TrustyAI | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E004 | Out-of-the-box standard benchmark suites | Implementation | TrustyAI | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E005 | Dashboard evaluation experience: launch, run status, scores, version comparison | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E006 | Evaluation step in Data Science Pipelines | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | P1 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E007 | Llama Stack eval provider backed by the platform evaluation service | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-E008 | Evaluation workflow documentation (incl. custom task authoring) | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E009 | Evaluation execution and result persistence on the LMEvalJob path (conditional, outcome A) | Implementation | TrustyAI | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E009 | Evaluation execution and result persistence via a new evaluation service (conditional, outcome B) | Implementation | TrustyAI | P0 |\n29\t\n30\tBranch epics are conditional alternatives selected by E001's outcome; they are plan\n31\tdocuments attached to the investigation, not standalone main-plan nodes.\n32\t`epic_count` = 8 main-plan epics (10 including branch alternatives).\n33\t\n34\t## Dependency DAG\n35\t\n36\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (\"X --> Y\" = X must complete before Y starts).\n37\t\n38\t```mermaid\n39\tgraph TD\n40\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: execution engine\"] --> E005[\"E005 Dashboard evaluation experience\"]\n41\t    E001 --> E006[\"E006 Pipelines evaluation step\"]\n42\t    E001 --> E007[\"E007 Llama Stack eval provider\"]\n43\t    E001 --> BE009[\"BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E009 (conditional execution core)\"]\n44\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: result schema\"] --> E005\n45\t    E002 --> E007\n46\t    E002 --> BE009\n47\t    E003[\"E003 Investigation: suite licensing\"] --> E004[\"E004 Out-of-the-box benchmark suites\"]\n48\t    E004 --> E008[\"E008 Documentation\"]\n49\t    E005 --> E008\n50\t    E006 --> E008\n51\t    E007 --> E008\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tMain-plan critical path length: 3 (e.g. E001 → E005 → E008, or E003 → E004 → E008).\n55\tBranch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n56\t\n57\t## DAG Justification\n58\t\n59\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n60\t|------|------|-----------|\n61\t| E001 → E005, E001 → E006, E001 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates) | The execution-engine decision defines the interface each consumer builds against; if a new service is chosen, these epics' integration approach is rewritten. True gates (`rewrite`). |\n62\t| E001 → BRANCH-A/B-E009 | Rule 3 (bounded outcomes → conditional branches) | The investigation has two bounded outcomes; the execution core epic exists only as branch alternatives. |\n63\t| E002 → E005, E002 → E007, E002 → BRANCH-A/B-E009 | Rules 3, 13 | The result schema is the data contract the Dashboard display/comparison, the Llama Stack provider results, and the persistence code build against — serial, true gate (`rewrite`). |\n64\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates) | Licensing determines which suites are bundled vs customer-fetched — changes what the suite epic delivers (`rewrite`). |\n65\t| E004 → E008, E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation is blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic; docs describe what was built. |\n66\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 (no edges) | — | Three independent unknowns; separate Investigation epics (Step 3: distinct deliverables, not split for the sake of splitting). |\n67\t| E004 ∥ E005 ∥ E006 ∥ E007 ∥ BRANCH-E009 (no edges between them) | Rules 14, 15 | Different components/repos and teams; parallel work once their gates resolve. |\n68\t| No edge E004 → BRANCH-E009 | Rule 13 (configuration references are AC gates) | Benchmark suites are runtime inputs (lm-eval task names), not build-against artifacts — the execution epic gets a \"selected suite available\" AC, not a dependency. |\n69\t\n70\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n71\t\n72\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n73\t|-----|----------|---------|\n74\t| HLR-1 (launch evaluation of served model against suite) | P0 | BRANCH-A/B-E009 (execution backend), E005 (Dashboard launch UI) |\n75\t| HLR-2 (standard suites out of the box) | P0 | E004 |\n76\t| HLR-3 (results persisted, linked to Model Registry version) | P0 | BRANCH-A/B-E009 (persistence), schema from E002 |\n77\t| HLR-4 (custom evaluation tasks through same flow) | P1 | BRANCH-A/B-E009 (bundled — shares the same execution surface) |\n78\t| HLR-5 (Dashboard: runs, status, scores, version comparison) | P1 | E005 |\n79\t| HLR-6 (evaluation as pipeline step, promotion gating) | P1 | E006 |\n80\t| HLR-7 (Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform) | P1 | E007 |\n81\t| HLR-8 (cost/duration metadata recording) | P2 | BRANCH-A/B-E009 (bundled — recorded by the same run execution) |\n82\t| HLR-9 (end-to-end product documentation) | P2 | E008 |\n83\t\n84\tAll 9 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n85\t\n86\t## Component Mapping Notes\n87\t\n88\t- All `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n89\t- **Model Registry is passive** in this strategy: custom properties on model versions\n90\t  are the existing extension point (per architecture context), so no Model Registry\n91\t  code epic is generated. Its involvement is carried by the E002 schema\n92\t  investigation (owned by the Model Registry team) plus persistence ACs on the\n93\t  execution epics.\n94\t- **KServe** is referenced by HLR-1 only as an evaluation target (served endpoint);\n95\t  it is not in the strategy's Affected Components and needs no changes — no epic.\n96\t- The branch execution epics are assigned component `TrustyAI`: even under outcome B\n97\t  (new evaluation service), the investigation scopes it to the TrustyAI component's\n98\t  midstream repo unless a new repo is recommended (which would add onboarding epics\n99\t  to that branch).\n100\t- **Documentation** (E008) is in the canonical component list; no architecture\n101\t  context file exists for it, which is expected for docs-authoring work.\n102\t\n103\t## Priority Notes\n104\t\n105\t- E001/E002/E003 are P0 by priority inheritance: they gate P0 work (HLR-1/2/3).\n106\t- E005 is P0: it carries the Dashboard launch UI for HLR-1 (P0) bundled with HLR-5\n107\t  (P1) — same Dashboard surface, so the P1 display/comparison work is not split out\n108\t  (Step 4: tightly coupled surface stays bundled).\n109\t- HLR-4 (P1) and HLR-8 (P2) are bundled into the execution epics: both run through\n110\t  the same execution surface / run recording as the P0 work and are not\n111\t  independently shippable surfaces.\n112\t- E008 (docs-authoring) is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy\n113\t  frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0); per the exception it does not push\n114\t  priority upstream.\n115\t\n116\t## Health Warnings\n117\t\n118\t1. **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table,\n119\t   Dependencies table, Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, or Non-Functional\n120\t   Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture-context owning teams; epic\n121\t   ACs were derived from HLRs. Non-blocking, but the refinement record is thinner\n122\t   than the template implies.\n123\t2. **Passive-component assumption**: Model Registry is treated as requiring no code\n124\t   changes (custom properties as extension point). If the schema investigation\n125\t   (E002) finds registry-side changes are needed (e.g. property size/indexing\n126\t   limits), a Model Registry implementation epic must be added.\n127\t3. **Scope trap (licensing)**: if most standard suites turn out non-redistributable,\n128\t   the out-of-the-box value of HLR-2 shrinks to a small catalog and the\n129\t   customer-fetched delivery mechanism carries the weight. The E003 gate makes this\n130\t   visible before the suite epic is built.\n131\t4. **Branch-B repo risk**: if the execution-engine investigation recommends a new\n132\t   repository for a standalone evaluation service, the outcome-B branch must be\n133\t   extended with repo-onboarding + license-validation → konflux-onboarding epics\n134\t   (Rule 20) before its implementation epic.\n135\t\n136\t## Tiered Delivery\n137\t\n138\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing (the three\n139\tunknowns are all resolvable investigations with bounded outcomes, not\n140\tschedule-gated upstream work). The execution-engine uncertainty is handled by\n141\tconditional branch decomposition rather than tiered delivery.\n142\t\n143\t## Conditional Branches\n144\t\n145\tE001 has two bounded outcomes; the execution core epic exists only as branch\n146\talternatives attached to the investigation:\n147\t\n148\t- **Branch A** (`RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E009`): the recommendation is to build on\n149\t  TrustyAI's LMEvalJob as the single execution path (extended as needed). The epic\n150\t  extends the existing LMEvalJob flow: launch against served models, custom tasks,\n151\t  result persistence, cost metadata.\n152\t- **Branch B** (`RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E009`): the recommendation is a new\n153\t  evaluation service (also backing the Llama Stack eval provider API). The epic\n154\t  builds that service with the same functional scope.\n155\t\n156\tConsumers of the execution surface (Dashboard, pipeline step, Llama Stack provider)\n157\tare main-plan epics that depend on the investigation outcome (E001), never on a\n158\tbranch epic — per the conditional-decomposition rules.\n159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83314622072566be","agent_id":"aaf5de986d62553a8","session_id":"a8243e44-1386-4db8-ba04-4dee4477cf9e","uuid":"238f6fa1-c27e-458f-8e88-f5dcf1741d72","timestamp":"2026-10-22T17:54:24.879Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9480b7e6d2755242","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
