om-task support and cost metadata run through the same\nlaunch/persistence surface and are not independently shippable surfaces.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\nNo circular dependencies. Diagram and frontmatter match in both directions:\nevery diagram edge (E001→E005/E006/E007/BE009, E002→E005/E006/E007/BE009,\nE003→E004, E004/E005/E006/E007→E008) has a corresponding entry in the target\nepic's `dependencies`, and no frontmatter dependency is absent from the\ndiagram or references a nonexistent epic. Arrows follow the dependency→\ndependent convention. Branch handling is correct: both branch files set\n`branch` (A/B, matching filenames) and `gated_by: E001`, the gate is in their\ndirect `dependencies`, and no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in\n`dependencies`. No konflux-onboarding/license-validation/repo-onboarding epics\nexist, so the rule 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are vacuously satisfied.\nRule 11 docs-last edges are complete (E008 blocked by all four main-plan\nImplementation epics). The E002→consumer edges are properly justified as Rule\n13 data-contract dependencies, and the suite-availability concern is correctly\nhandled as an AC gate (\"selected out-of-the-box suites are available to the\nexecution path\") rather than a `dependencies` edge, per the\nconfiguration-reference rule. Critical path 3 (main plan) is reasonable for a\nstrategy of this size given the deliberate parallelism. Two minor notes:\n(a) E004's suite-identity/version recording and E002's schema share the \"suite\nidentity\" concept with no coordination edge or AC — both derive from lm-eval\ntask naming, so no build-against artifact flows, but an explicit AC tying the\ncatalog identity to the schema would remove the ambiguity; (b) E008's\ndependence on the eventual execution core (branch epic) is only implicit —\ncorrectly so, since a main-plan→branch edge is forbidden — and should be made\nexplicit by adding the selected branch epics to E008's dependencies at\nresolution time.\n\n### 